.Dd November 17, 2026
.Dt TX 1
.Os zdots
.Sh NAME
.Nm tx
.Nd one way into tmux sessions from a shell, inside tmux, or a terminal launch
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar session
.Nm
.Cm -
.Nm
.Fl -landing
.Nm
.Op Fl h | Fl -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
attaches to tmux sessions the same way everywhere.
.Pp
With a
.Ar session
name it attaches to that session, creating it in the current directory if it
does not exist. Inside tmux it switches the current client instead, because
tmux refuses to start a session inside another one.
.Pp
With no arguments it opens the session picker inside tmux, or lists the
sessions outside it.
.Pp
With
.Cm -
it switches back to the previous session (inside tmux only).
.Pp
.Fl -landing
is for terminal emulator launch commands (iTerm2's durable tmux profile).
The first window attaches to the landing session.
Every later window or tab gets a new tmux window in that session, shown through
its own grouped view
.Pq Ao landing Ac Ns -N ,
so it survives a terminal restart like the rest of tmux.
Unattached grouped views are pruned the next time a view is opened, while their
windows stay in the landing session.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Fl
.It Fl -landing
Attach to the landing session, or open a new window via a grouped view
if a client already shows it.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Show usage.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width ZDOTS_TMUX_LANDING
.It Ev ZDOTS_TMUX_LANDING
Landing session name. Default:
.Ql main .
.It Ev TX_TMUX
tmux binary or wrapper script. Default:
.Ql tmux .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tx                 # pick a session (inside tmux) or list them
tx notes           # go to the notes session, creating it here if needed
tx --landing       # in a terminal profile's launch command
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr tmux 1
.Pp
The tmux configuration and key bindings
.Pq prefix N, prefix s, prefix Backspace
are documented in
.Pa ~/.config/tmux/README.md .
